  - `npm run docs:check`, a CI job and a GitHub Pages workflow;
  - `docs/writing-docs.md`, the guide every writer follows.
- 2026-10-19: Tutorial 1's steps ran from a fresh clone on a local Podman stack: the isolation check passed at start, and the smoke check passed with all 12 candidates judged as expected in about 100 seconds on a laptop CPU.
- 2026-10-19: Step 3 done. Nine writers wrote the 66 remaining pages from the inventories and the code, each with a file of evidence and a file of gaps.
- 2026-10-19: Step 4, checking:
  - **Against the code.** Fresh verifiers checked every page's claims against the code, ran a Diátaxis audit and read for plain words. They corrected about 175 claims, fixed the pages that mixed types, and took every description of a security weakness off the pages.
  - **Fresh readers.** Two readers that saw only the built site tried every how-to guide (139 findings) and every tutorial, with the home page and Start here (45 findings). Six fixers fixed each finding or recorded why it needed no change, and made the pages agree with each other and with the code on the words they use.
  - **Tutorials, run end to end** on a clean local stack (Podman, from a fresh clone, images from the build cache): 1 Your first run, about 5 minutes after the build; 2 Hand the work to agents, about 30 minutes with a free model; 3 Your first goal campaign, about 15 minutes, no model; 4 Your own project, about 30 minutes, against a throwaway public test repository. Each page was corrected to what happened. The tutorials' order is now 1, 2, 3 (goal campaign), 4 (own project).
  - **How-to guides, followed once** on local stacks of their own:
    - ran fully: tokens, the REST API, cancel a command, check a rig, accept a rig's key, the results repository (with a stand-in SSH Git host), stop, restart, retire and fully reset a rig, write a contract, test a kit, steer a run, budgets and stop times, take the result, run a campaign on several rigs, write a kit, install on one machine, push the images to your own registry (a local registry), extend the harness image;
    - ran in part: let an agent run Thermite over MCP (with curl, not a client's settings), start a campaign from the command line, decide what waits for you (promotions only), configure advisors (with a scripted model), connect a provider, choose a model and give each agent its own model (free models were rate-limited; a stand-in model server did the rest), and troubleshoot (spot checks);
    - through the repository's stand-ins: add a rig on a machine (the rig deploy acceptance), add a rig on a cluster (the cluster acceptance on kind), join a rig yourself (a stand-in machine, and a namespace on kind), give a rig several GPUs (the multi-GPU acceptance on fake GPUs);
    - not run: test a GPU on RunPod (a paid service), the Claude and ChatGPT subscriptions (the owner's accounts), a private repository (a private repository on a Git host), and providers that need a paid key.
  - **Gaps.** What the code lacks went into goal [061](../061-gaps-found-by-the-docs.goal): 164 items. Two of them were code bugs found by running the guides, now fixed on the development branch: rigs on machines that never reported (`4ec8705`), and the planner under OpenCode (`9a8f803`). Security items went to the maintainers' private notes only.
